---
title: "Read this first"
description: "What this book is for, who wrote it, and what deliberately lives somewhere else."
---

You have inherited a Telegram bot that runs an eight-week physical activity
competition between Aalto University student guilds. It is small: about 1,950
effective lines across 25 TypeScript files, two containers, one process, one
database. It is also unusually opinionated for its size, and most of the
opinions are load-bearing rather than stylistic.

This book is the orientation the repository does not give you. It explains the
shape of the thing, why each load-bearing decision is what it is, and how to run,
change and debug it.

## What is not here, on purpose

The repository already documents itself well, and duplicating any of it would
guarantee the copies drift apart. This book cites those documents rather than
restating them.

| Question | Where it is answered |
|---|---|
| What must the bot do? | `SPEC.md` §5, the numbered FR-1 to FR-27 and NFR-1 to NFR-6 list. **This is the source of truth.** |
| Why is some obvious feature missing? | `SPEC.md` §8, "Rejected alternatives". Read it before adding anything. |
| What was decided during each build phase? | `docs/superpowers/specs/*-phase-N-design.md`, four documents. Code comments cite them as "design 4.x" and "phase N design N.N". |
| What evidence backs the scoring model? | `docs/evidence.md`, with primary citations and the claims that did not survive checking. |
| How do I accept a phase as working? | `docs/SMOKE.md`, the manual checklist. It is the only acceptance basis for the Telegram-facing files. |
| What does each screen look like? | `prototype/bot-flows.html`, **which has been deleted.** Several documents still link to it. Recover it with `git show 77507ee^:prototype/bot-flows.html`. See [Shape](orientation/shape.tmd). |
| What will bite me editing a specific file? | `CLAUDE.md`, written for a reader already inside the code. |

When this book and the code disagree, the code is right and this book has rotted.
When this book and `SPEC.md` disagree about a requirement, `SPEC.md` is right.

## How to read it, by what you came to do

::: {.callout-tip}
## Running next year's competition

Read [The competition](orientation/the-competition.tmd) for the model, then go
straight to [Next season](ops/next-season.tmd). [Configuration](code/config.tmd)
explains why a single file edit recomputes all of history, which is the property
the runbook depends on.
:::

::: {.callout-tip}
## Changing or extending the code

Read [Shape](orientation/shape.tmd), then [Invariants](invariants.tmd) before you
touch anything. The invariants chapter is not general advice: every rule in it
has already caused or nearly caused a defect, and each one is stated with the
defect as evidence. Then read whichever layer chapter covers your change.
:::

::: {.callout-tip}
## Debugging it in production

[The ticker](code/ticker.tmd) explains everything that happens without a user
action, which is where most confusing behaviour comes from.
[Troubleshooting](ops/troubleshooting.tmd) is a symptom-to-cause table with the
query or command that confirms each one.
:::

## The one thing to know before anything else

**No total is ever stored.** The database records which of four tiers a person
tapped on a given day, and nothing else about their activity. Every minute
figure, every guild ranking, every streak and every percentage in the product is
derived at read time from that plus `src/config.ts`.

This is why the next-season runbook is mostly a config edit, why a corrected
member count fixes history instead of only the future, and why several of the
invariants exist. It is `SPEC.md` §4.4, and it is the assumption underneath the
rest of this book.
